":0.2845,"shared":4,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Broncos are 8-2 through week 11 of the 2026 season and have outscored opponents 236 to 210. The offense ranks 15th in EPA per play and the defense 15th in EPA allowed, so neither side has stood out much yet.","offense":"The Broncos offense has been about average by EPA per play so far. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Bo Nix has run the passing game, with Jaylen Waddle leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Broncos defense has been about average by EPA allowed per play so far. It has been much better against the pass than the run, ranking 14th and 27th. Nik Bonitto has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Malcolm Roach, who has 5 sacks despite playing only 57% of the snaps. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a blitz rate that ranks 11th highest in the league.","special":"The Broncos have been one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 27% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers have made 85% of field goals, which is about average. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. Their offensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Bo Nix has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,955 yards. Targets have been spread around, with Jaylen Waddle and Courtland Sutton leading a group where nobody has more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield has been a committee led by J.K. Dobbins and Jonah Coleman. On defense, Zach Allen leads the team with 5 sacks and Brandon Jones has picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Davis Webb is calling the offense and Vance Joseph is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2022 Packers under Matt LaFleur and the 2022 Jaguars. Their defense looks most like the 2023 Chiefs under Steve Spagnuolo and the 2025 Patriots.","trends":"The offense went from 10th in EPA per play in 2025 to 15th so far this season, a modest decline. The defense moved from 6th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 15th so far this season, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was pre-snap motion rate, which went from 47% to 62%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}